WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.337
Seven o'clock on moving day.

2
00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:07.846
The truck is in the street, and the packers are in the hall.

3
00:00:07.846 --> 00:00:11.588
But the driver says: I'll leave home when the truck picks me up.

4
00:00:11.588 --> 00:00:14.161
And the truck can't move without its driver.

5
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:15.462
So nobody moves.

6
00:00:15.462 --> 00:00:18.817
And the mistake was on the plan the night before.

7
00:00:19.733 --> 00:00:22.868
Last lesson, KSP wrote that plan during the build.

8
00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:27.820
A walk visits each class, and writes it on the sheet after everything it needs.

9
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.017
Here's the driver who waits for the truck, in code.

10
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:34.095
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:34.095 --> 00:00:37.306
The walk already remembers every class it has finished.

12
00:00:37.306 --> 00:00:40.576
Isn't that enough to notice it's going round in circles?

13
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.145
Let's watch it go wrong on the day first.

14
00:00:46.145 --> 00:00:55.029
A reflection container, like the one you built in Part 4, reads your classes while the program runs, and builds each constructor's arguments first.

15
00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:58.629
Each call that hasn't finished yet keeps a stack frame.

16
00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:03.631
The truck asks for a driver, the driver asks for a truck, and the frames pile up.

17
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:11.673
It ends in a StackOverflowError, with 1,024 frames: as many as the JVM keeps in a stack trace by default.

18
00:01:11.673 --> 00:01:16.302
That's the container asking itself for a truck, over and over.

19
00:01:16.302 --> 00:01:19.064
The longest phone call nobody wanted.

20
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:22.939
Lesson 1's processor fails the same way, only earlier.

21
00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:29.196
In a test compile, it stops with INTERNAL_ERROR, and a StackOverflowError inside KotlinPoet.

22
00:01:29.196 --> 00:01:30.421
Earlier is better.

23
00:01:30.421 --> 00:01:33.821
But neither message names the truck or the driver.

24
00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:37.398
So no, remembering what's finished isn't enough.

25
00:01:37.398 --> 00:01:40.912
When the driver asks for the truck, the truck isn't finished.

26
00:01:40.912 --> 00:01:42.525
The walk is still inside it.

27
00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:45.741
Remember the planner's phone round from the last video?

28
00:01:45.741 --> 00:01:49.514
You call the moving day, and keep it on hold while you call the crew.

29
00:01:49.514 --> 00:01:54.380
The crew stays on hold while you call the truck, and the truck while you call the driver.

30
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:55.420
Let's map it.

31
00:01:55.420 --> 00:02:00.592
The calls on hold are the path: the classes the walk has entered and not yet left.

32
00:02:00.592 --> 00:02:02.610
The names on the sheet are done.

33
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.196
Lesson 1's walk only looked at the sheet.

34
00:02:05.196 --> 00:02:06.836
Look at the hold list too.

35
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:07.899
Let's run it.

36
00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:12.954
The walk enters the moving day, then the crew, then the truck, then the driver.

37
00:02:12.954 --> 00:02:15.897
The path reads MovingDay, Crew, Truck, Driver.

38
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.289
The driver's first need is the route.

39
00:02:18.289 --> 00:02:21.660
It needs nothing, so it goes straight on the sheet: done.

40
00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:23.685
His second need is the truck.

41
00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:26.381
Is the truck on the sheet, on hold, or new?

42
00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:30.062
On hold.

43
00:02:30.062 --> 00:02:31.982
The truck is still on the path.

44
00:02:31.982 --> 00:02:35.450
The walk has come back to something it's still building.

45
00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:36.441
That's the loop.

46
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:39.003
To report it, cut the path at the truck.

47
00:02:39.003 --> 00:02:44.083
Drop everything before it, and add the truck once more: Truck needs Driver needs Truck.

48
00:02:44.083 --> 00:02:48.463
The moving day and the crew lead into the loop, but they aren't part of it.

49
00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:53.393
And the error goes on the driver's truck parameter, the arrow that closes the loop.

50
00:02:53.393 --> 00:02:54.931
That's the line you'd edit.

51
00:02:56.233 --> 00:02:58.549
This has a name: a dependency cycle.

52
00:02:58.549 --> 00:03:02.344
A path of needs arrows that comes back to where it started.

53
00:03:02.344 --> 00:03:08.840
Nothing in a cycle can be built first, because each constructor needs the other object already built.

54
00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:11.752
In the real build, it reads like this.

55
00:03:11.752 --> 00:03:16.628
Driver.kt, line 12: Dependency cycle: Truck needs Driver needs Truck.

56
00:03:16.628 --> 00:03:21.716
Nothing in a cycle can be built first: change one of these constructors.

57
00:03:21.933 --> 00:03:24.771
Notice it doesn't blame the driver, or the truck.

58
00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:27.320
It hands you the list of suspects, in order.

59
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.868
Each name is a constructor you could change.

60
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.976
Now, here's a shortcut you might try: if I've met this class before, it's a loop.

61
00:03:35.976 --> 00:03:37.254
Let's be fair to it.

62
00:03:37.254 --> 00:03:39.299
On this crew, it finds the loop.

63
00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:41.216
But try it on lesson 1's crew.

64
00:03:42.233 --> 00:03:46.097
There, the driver and the moving day both need the route.

65
00:03:46.097 --> 00:03:49.011
The walk finishes the route for the driver.

66
00:03:49.011 --> 00:03:51.722
Later, the moving day asks for it again.

67
00:03:51.722 --> 00:03:53.349
Met before, so: a cycle?

68
00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:53.552
No.

69
00:03:53.552 --> 00:03:56.737
Two arrows into one box is sharing, not a loop.

70
00:03:56.967 --> 00:03:59.889
So the walk asks its questions in order.

71
00:03:59.889 --> 00:04:01.277
Nobody provides it?

72
00:04:01.277 --> 00:04:04.930
That's a missing binding, from the section before.

73
00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.464
Already on the sheet?

74
00:04:06.464 --> 00:04:07.852
Shared, so skip it.

75
00:04:07.852 --> 00:04:08.437
On hold?

76
00:04:08.437 --> 00:04:10.044
A cycle, so report it.

77
00:04:10.044 --> 00:04:11.505
Otherwise, visit it.

78
00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:15.467
Here's the cycle check in Kotlin.

79
00:04:15.467 --> 00:04:23.047
If the target is on the path, drop everything before it, add it once more, and report the loop on the parameter that asked.

80
00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:29.247
And its Java twin, a javac processor doing the same check with its list called path.

81
00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:31.542
Here the picture breaks.

82
00:04:31.542 --> 00:04:36.892
On a real street, the driver and the truck company would sort it out with a phone call.

83
00:04:36.892 --> 00:04:38.184
Code can't negotiate.

84
00:04:38.184 --> 00:04:40.397
Someone has to change a constructor.

85
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:45.367
Lesson 3 meets Lazy, which waits to build something until it's first used.

86
00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:48.974
Our processor still calls a loop through a Lazy a cycle.

87
00:04:48.974 --> 00:04:50.327
It's the safe answer.

88
00:04:51.233 --> 00:04:53.776
So, is remembering what's finished enough?

89
00:04:53.776 --> 00:04:53.958
No.

90
00:04:53.958 --> 00:04:57.409
A cycle is a request for something you're still building.

91
00:04:57.409 --> 00:05:00.133
Keep the path, and check it before you visit.

92
00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.979
Then the build names the loop the night before.

93
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:03.943
Quick check.

94
00:05:03.943 --> 00:05:07.098
Instead of the truck, the driver asks for the crew.

95
00:05:07.098 --> 00:05:08.831
What does the loop read now?

96
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.685
Crew needs Truck needs Driver needs Crew.

97
00:05:14.685 --> 00:05:20.514
The error is on line 12 of Driver.kt again, because that parameter still closes the loop.

98
00:05:20.514 --> 00:05:23.199
The loop just starts further up the path.
